{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785296535,"nonce":"W21GYYLizimo7jhOVIB/Vrwvo70=","message":"RCA:\n\nCode path: src/b4/review_tui/_review_app.py:1342-1350 (action_edit_reply stores result.decode() verbatim), src/b4/__init__.py:6091 (edit_in_editor, bytes-faithful round trip), src/b4/review/_review.py:2636 (_insert_trailer_in_reply), _review.py:2657 (_remove_trailer_from_reply).\n\nProven in code:\n- review['reply'] is only ever written from editor output (two sites: _review_app.py:1350 and the trailer-menu rewrite at _review_app.py:1169); there is no lore-import path for it. So the \\r bytes entered via the editor writing the temp file with CRLF line endings, and b4 performs no normalization anywhere on the read-back path.\n- Once the stored buffer is CRLF, _insert_trailer_in_reply and _remove_trailer_from_reply split on '\\n' and join with '\\n': existing lines keep their trailing \\r while an inserted trailer line (e.g. Reviewed-by via the trailer menu) is LF-only. The result is a mixed-ending buffer. On the next edit, vim cannot detect fileformat=dos for mixed endings, opens the file as unix, and displays ^M on every original line — exactly the reported symptom, including the freshly inserted Reviewed-by line being the one without ^M.\n- The 'extra new lines due to all these \\r' in the email-mode view follow from the \\r characters surviving into the rendered preview.\n\nInferred (not proven): why the reporter's editor saved CRLF in the first place. The temp file is named reply.b4-review.eml (b4.edit_in_editor filehint); .eml files are conventionally CRLF (RFC 5322 / Windows mail exports) and mail-oriented editor configs commonly force fileformat=dos for them. The reporter's exact editor configuration cannot be verified from here, so lifecycle:confirmed is withheld.\n\nRegardless of origin, the buffer is documented as the source of truth and everything downstream assumes LF; normalizing \\r\\n and \\r to \\n when reading the editor result back is a lossless canonicalization point.\n\nRelated: the disappearing 'Fixes:' line from the same report is a separate defect (trailer menu deletes unmanaged trailers from reply buffer, 9ef6d0d).","files":null}]}